Database migrations — policy and workflow
==========================================

Alembic is the official mechanism for every schema change in this project:
new tables, columns, foreign keys, indexes, constraints, type changes,
nullable changes, renames, and data migrations. If you're changing a model
in app/models/, you almost certainly need a new revision here too.

Configuration
-------------
- `alembic/env.py` reads the database URL from
  `app.core.config.settings.sqlalchemy_database_url` — the exact same URL
  the running FastAPI app connects with (including the `postgres://` ->
  `postgresql+psycopg://` normalization Render's connection strings need).
  There is no second, separately-configured database URL anywhere in this
  setup.
- `target_metadata` is `app.core.database.Base.metadata`, populated by
  importing `app.models` (every model module). Autogenerate therefore
  compares against the real, current set of models — not a stale copy.
- In online mode, migrations run through `app.core.database.engine` itself
  (same pool config, same SQLite `check_same_thread` handling) rather than a
  second engine built from `alembic.ini`.

Everyday commands
------------------
    # See what would run, without touching the database:
    alembic history
    alembic current

    # Apply all pending migrations:
    alembic upgrade head

    # Roll back the most recent migration:
    alembic downgrade -1

    # Create a new migration (review the autogenerated diff before committing —
    # autogenerate is a starting point, not a guarantee):
    alembic revision --autogenerate -m "add <thing>"

    # Or hand-write one with no autogenerate diff (e.g. a data migration, or
    # anything needing the kind of defensive existence-checks the
    # quotations.lead_id migration uses):
    alembic revision -m "<description>"

Relationship to the pre-Alembic auto-migration helpers
--------------------------------------------------------
Before Alembic, this project's schema was created and evolved entirely by
`Base.metadata.create_all()` plus a set of hand-written, self-auditing
compatibility functions in `app/core/database.py`
(`auto_add_missing_columns`, `extend_pg_enum_types`,
`relax_not_null_columns`, `widen_columns_to_text`,
`add_columns_with_default`, `drop_legacy_columns`, `enforce_unique_email`,
`enforce_unique_google_id`) — all still run at application startup, and all
still work exactly as before.

**They are now frozen** — see that module's top docstring for the full
reasoning. In short: they are kept running only as a compatibility net for
a database that hasn't had `alembic upgrade head` run against it yet, and
for local dev / the test suite (which import the app without ever invoking
Alembic). No new entries should be added to them; new schema work is an
Alembic revision instead.

The one exception, `Base.metadata.create_all()` itself, stays in the
startup path unmodified: it is purely additive (creates a table only if
missing, never alters an existing one), and this project's own Alembic
baseline revision (`16168c6e504c_baseline_schema_create_all.py`) calls the
exact same function, so the two can never disagree with each other.

Adopting Alembic against an already-running database
-------------------------------------------------------
No `alembic stamp` step is needed. Because the baseline revision wraps
`create_all()` (a safe no-op against tables that already exist) and every
later revision guards each operation with an explicit existence check
against the live schema (see `8175f96a08e4_quotations_lead_id_fk.py` for the
pattern), `alembic upgrade head` is safe to run as the very first Alembic
command ever run against this project's existing dev or production
database — it detects what's already there and only does the work that's
actually missing.

Writing a new migration safely
--------------------------------
Follow the pattern in `8175f96a08e4_quotations_lead_id_fk.py`:
1. Inspect the live schema with `sqlalchemy.inspect(op.get_bind())` before
   each operation — never assume a fresh database vs. an existing one.
2. Guard every `op.add_column` / `op.create_foreign_key` / `op.create_index`
   / etc. with an existence check, so re-running the migration (or running
   it against a database that already has the change, e.g. a fresh one
   created by the baseline revision) is a safe no-op, not an error.
3. If a new FK could have orphaned references on an existing database
   (rows written before the constraint existed), clean them up explicitly
   — log what was changed and why — before adding the constraint.
4. SQLite cannot add a constraint to an existing table via a plain
   `ALTER TABLE`. Use `op.batch_alter_table(...)` there; plain
   `op.create_foreign_key(...)` on Postgres.
5. Write a `downgrade()` that only reverses what *this* migration is
   guaranteed to have added. Don't drop a column in `downgrade()` if an
   existing database might have had that column (with real data in it)
   before this migration ever ran — that turns a rollback into silent data
   loss on some deployments and a no-op on others, which is far worse than
   an asymmetric but honest downgrade.

Production deployment (Render)
--------------------------------
Migrations run as an explicit, separate release step — never implicitly on
every application boot. See `render.yaml`'s `preDeployCommand`
(`alembic upgrade head`), which Render runs once per deploy, before the new
app version starts serving traffic, and does not run redundantly per
instance if the service scales to multiple web processes.

If a migration fails, the deploy stops before the new code goes live — the
previous release keeps serving traffic. Take a database backup (Render's
point-in-time recovery / manual backup) before any migration that isn't a
purely additive, easily-reversible change, exactly as you would with any
other production schema change.
